---
import ToolLayout from "../../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro";
import { CompressorTool } from "../../../components/image/CompressorTool.js";
import { getToolByPath } from "../../../content/tools.js";

const tool = getToolByPath("/tools/image/compressor")!;
---

<ToolLayout
  tool={tool}
  description="Reduce image file size with an adjustable JPEG or WebP quality slider, processed in your browser."
  purpose="Reduce a PNG, JPG, or WebP image's file size by re-encoding it as JPEG or WebP at an adjustable quality level."
>
  <CompressorTool client:load />

  <Fragment slot="details">
    <h2>How it works</h2>
    <p>
      Select an image and a target format (JPEG or WebP), then adjust the quality slider. Lower quality produces a
      smaller file at the cost of visible compression artifacts (blurring, blockiness, or color banding).
    </p>

    <h2>Worked example</h2>
    <p>
      A 3 MB PNG photo compressed to JPEG at quality 75 might result in a 300–500 KB file with little visible
      difference; at quality 20, the file shrinks further but artifacts become noticeable.
    </p>

    <h2>Assumptions and limitations</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Quality settings influence file size but do not guarantee a specific output size — the relationship depends on image content.</li>
      <li>This is lossy compression: reduced quality is not reversible, and repeated recompression compounds quality loss.</li>
      <li>PNG output is not offered here because PNG compression is lossless and doesn't have a comparable "quality" tradeoff — use the Image Resizer or a dedicated PNG optimizer for that.</li>
      <li>Transparent areas are filled with your chosen background color only when compressing to JPEG (WebP keeps transparency).</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Local vs. server processing</h2>
    <p>
      Compression runs on your device by default. The explicit server option re-encodes the file with Sharp on our
      API and deletes it afterward.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
